## The seven gaps

### 1. The measurement layer cannot measure what the strategy layer promises

VIRL tells the model, every week, that success in real estate is *"DMs and
profile visits (the real pipeline signal)… listing/buyer consult requests
started from social"* (`niche-playbook.js:40-45`), and that at least one success
metric *"must be a business signal tied to the PRIMARY goal… not raw reach"*
(`prompts.js:1501`).

Then the only thing a creator can log is **views, likes, saves**
(`index.html:4657`):

```js
function saveResults() { onLog(card,{views:views,likes:likes,saves:saves2}); }
```

It's worse than an omission. The screenshot OCR **already extracts comments and
shares** (`prompts.js:2246-2249`) and the client silently drops both
(`index.html:4670-4674`). Data captured, parsed, and discarded at the point of
capture.

Every downstream consumer inherits the ceiling: `engagementScore` in
`performance-insights.js:47`, `plan-history.js:98`, and `vault-exemplars.js:31`
all define engagement as `views + likes×2 + saves×4`.

**Consequence:** VIRL sets business-outcome goals and grades itself on vanity
metrics. The renewal conversation is "you got 42,000 views" — which the creator
can already see for free inside Instagram. The renewal conversation that wins is
"you started 6 consult conversations from social in 60 days, 4 of them from
market-data posts."

**Fix:** extend the result shape to `comments`, `shares`, `follows`,
`profileVisits`, `linkClicks` (OCR already returns two; the rest are visible in
the same native insights panel), plus one manual, high-value field — *"business
outcome from this post"* (DM inquiry / consult booked / sale / list signup),
logged as a count with an optional note. Update the engagement weights, and let
the niche playbook's own KPI list drive which fields are prompted per niche.

**Effort:** small. **Impact:** this is the change that makes the subscription
defensible.

---

### 2. The strategy is never graded

Every plan opens with a `strategy` object: a thesis, a bet, and 3–4 concrete
success metrics with thresholds (`prompts.js:1492-1502`). This is the product's
most differentiated artifact.

Nothing ever scores it. `plan-history.js:109-138` renders the prior weeks'
strategy, top performer, and card list into the next prompt — but there is no
comparison of last week's `success_metric` targets against actual logged
numbers, no verdict field, and nothing the creator ever sees.

So VIRL makes a specific, falsifiable prediction every single week and then
quietly declines to check it. That's not a strategist. A strategist's entire
value is the retrospective.

**Fix:** a weekly **Scorecard**. On plan generation, compute last week's
`success_metric` targets vs. logged actuals, have the model write a 2–3 sentence
verdict in the VIRL persona ("The carousel bet paid — 3 of 4 metrics hit. The
Thursday listing post underperformed your average, which tracks: convert posts
need a nurture post ahead of them"), persist it on `plan_history`, feed it into
the next prompt, **and show it to the creator as the first thing they see on
Monday.**

This is simultaneously the highest-value retention surface in the product (a
weekly reason to open the app that isn't "generate more content") and the
strongest possible proof the subscription is doing something. It also makes the
history block dramatically more useful to the model — a graded bet is far better
context than an ungraded one.

**Effort:** medium. **Impact:** highest of anything in this document.

---

### 3. The results loop learns *format* and *platform* — never topic, angle, or funnel stage

`computePerformanceInsights` (`performance-insights.js:76-104`) buckets logged
results by exactly two dimensions: `format` and `platform`. That's what the
"Proven for you" panel shows (`index.html:8491-8526`) and what
`formatPerformanceForPrompt` feeds the model.

But the strategy layer chooses along entirely different axes: content pillars,
funnel stage (attract/nurture/convert, `niche-playbook.js:38`), hook archetype
(`prompts.js:849`), and topic. VIRL can tell a real estate agent "carousels
outperform your reels." It can never tell them *"your market-data posts
outperform your lifestyle posts 3:1 — and your convert posts only land when a
nurture post ran within 48 hours."*

That second sentence is the product. It's the thing a creator genuinely cannot
self-diagnose, and the reason to pay someone monthly.

**Fix:** emit `pillar`, `funnelStage`, and `hookArchetype` on every card (schema
addition; the model is already reasoning in these terms — `VARIETY_BLOCK` names
the archetypes explicitly and the niche funnel targets are already in-prompt).
Persist them on the vault row, bucket them in `performance-insights.js`, and
raise the honesty gates per-dimension the same way the existing ones work.

**Effort:** small-to-medium, and it compounds with #1. **Impact:** high — it's
the difference between analytics and insight.

---

### 4. "Never posted" is treated as "didn't work"

`prompts.js:577` instructs the model to *"avoid repeating themes that didn't
land or that the creator never logged."* `plan-history.js:136` counts
`unlogged`.

But unlogged overwhelmingly means **unshipped**, and unshipped is the biggest
value leak in the entire product: a plan the creator doesn't execute produces
exactly zero impact no matter how good it is. Conflating the two teaches the
model to retire ideas that may have been excellent but were too hard to make.

Worse, the server can't even tell the two apart. Posted state is
**localStorage-only** (`index.html:2656-2668`) — it doesn't survive a device
change, and no server-side consumer can distinguish "posted, never measured"
from "never made."

The codebase already half-suspects the real constraint. `filmDirection` requires
*"at least one option that works without the creator's face on camera (b-roll +
voiceover) so camera-shy days still ship"* (`prompts.js:1282`). That's a good
guess at a friction the product could simply **learn**.

**Fix:** (a) persist posted state server-side; (b) when a card is skipped, ask
one question — *too hard to film / didn't fit my week / didn't like the idea /
ran out of time* — and feed the answer into the next plan. "You've skipped every
card that needs you on camera three weeks running, so this week is b-roll and
carousels" is a strategist noticing something about you. It converts the
product's largest silent failure mode into its highest-value learning signal.

**Effort:** small. **Impact:** high — it attacks execution rate, which multiplies
every other improvement in this document.

---

### 5. Plan — the flagship surface — has no voice gate

There is a real voice-correction mechanism: when drift exceeds
`DRIFT_RETRY_THRESHOLD`, the generation is retried with a targeted per-axis
correction (`chat.js:1798-1849`, `voiceCorrectionInstruction` at `chat.js:64`).
It's well built.

It only runs on **non-streaming** generations. Plan streams
(`handleStreamingPlan`), so plan drift is computed and written to the usage row
(`chat.js:490-502`) and logged as telemetry (`chat.js:594-612`) — and then
nothing happens. The surface that costs 3 credits, produces ~14 cards, carries
the week, and is the product's centerpiece is the one surface where "sounds like
you" is measured and never enforced.

The mechanism to fix it already exists: the post-stream `compliance` event
(`chat.js:546-560`) parses the final text after the stream closes and pushes a
corrected payload to the client. A voice pass can ride the same channel.

**Fix:** after stream close, score drift per card; for the worst offenders only
(cap at 2–3), run a cheap targeted rewrite and push them over the existing
post-stream event path. Surface it honestly in the UI — *"tightened 3 cards to
your voice"* — which turns an invisible quality gate into visible evidence the
subscription is working.

**Effort:** medium. **Impact:** high — this is the core promise on the core
surface.

---

### 6. Voice learning is a rolling window, not an accumulating asset

Everything VIRL "learns" about voice expires:

- Edit diffs: last **8 diffs** from the last **12 events** (`edit-examples.js:29,49`)
- Reaction chips: **60-day** window, **3-word** vocabulary (`voice-reactions.js:46-55`)
- Voice fingerprint: computed **only** from user-authored samples, never updated
  from anything VIRL observes (`prompts.js:137-151`)
- Thumbs-**up** ratings: stored by `api/rate.js`, and read by exactly one
  consumer — `api/admin/business-stats.js`. **Never fed back into generation.**

Two consequences. First, the loop is asymmetric: VIRL learns only from what the
creator *dislikes*. The single strongest voice exemplar available — a draft the
creator explicitly marked as sounding right — is collected and discarded.
Second, and more damaging to the business model: **month-six VIRL is not
meaningfully smarter about your voice than month-one VIRL.** The window slides;
nothing accumulates. That is precisely the thing a subscription is supposed to
buy.

**Fix:** a durable, versioned **Voice Profile** — a learned artifact distilled
periodically (say monthly) from edits, reactions, up-rated drafts, and
high-performing posts into a compact set of standing rules. Three properties
matter: it *accumulates* (quality genuinely compounds), it's *visible* ("here's
what I've learned about how you write" is the best possible proof of value), and
it's *editable* (the creator can correct it, which makes it more accurate and
makes it theirs). Feed up-rated drafts in as positive exemplars alongside vault
saves.

This also creates the only real switching cost the product has. A competitor can
copy the prompts. They cannot copy nine months of your learned voice profile.

**Effort:** medium-to-large. **Impact:** high, and it is the most direct answer
to "why keep paying."

---

### 7. The algorithm research is invisible to the person paying for it

`cron/playbook-refresh.js` runs monthly per-platform research against a
trusted-source allowlist, produces a **per-field diff with source URLs and
reasoning** (`playbook-refresh.js:41-56`), and stages it for admin approval.
`trend-context.js` ingests trends twice weekly with lifecycle status and niche
fit scoring. `research-verify.js` fact-checks channel research before it reaches
the creator.

All of it dies in the admin dashboard. The creator never learns that any of it
happened.

For a product whose promise is "based on platform and algorithm best practices,"
invisible work is wasted work — it's paid for and never credited.

**Fix:** a monthly creator-facing note built from data already collected and
already structured: *"Instagram changed how Reels reach non-followers this
month. Here's what I changed in your plans, and here's the source."* The `diff`,
`reasoning`, and `sources` fields are sitting in `playbook_drafts` waiting to be
read.

**Effort:** small. **Impact:** medium-high, and disproportionate to the cost —
it converts existing infrastructure into visible subscription value.

