## MARKETING COUNCIL REVIEW — revisions before shipping

Four seats reviewed the roadmap above: brand, head of content, SEO/demand-gen,
and campaign/launch. They converged hard, and the priority table above does not
survive contact with them. What follows supersedes it.

### What the council changed my mind about

**1. The sequencing is wrong, unanimously.** All four seats independently
reached the same verdict: this roadmap improves month six and does nothing for
day three. `TRIAL_DAYS = 14` (`api/chat.js:37`) means a free user gets **two
Mondays**, and the first one has nothing logged — so the flagship Scorecard is
structurally incapable of producing a real verdict before the credit-card
decision. Items 1–4 make the product better for people who already paid, funded
by people who haven't.

**2. I built four recommendations on a loop whose throughput I never measured.**
The current results-logging rate appears nowhere in this document. It is
measurable *today*: `result_logged` (`index.html:11588`), `post_marked`
(`index.html:4701`), and `plan_generated` (`index.html:11231`) all fire into the
events table. `api/admin/business-stats.js` simply doesn't aggregate them. That's
a query, not a project, and it decides whether items 1–4 are worth a cycle.

**3. The logging loop may be broken, not merely unloved — and this is the most
actionable finding in the review.** Posted state is localStorage-only
(`index.html:2656-2668`), and the "Log results" button renders *only when
`posted` is true* (`index.html:4930`). An agent who plans on desktop and posts
from their phone sees "Mark as posted" instead — the state does not travel.
`countPostedLastWeekUnlogged` scans localStorage too, so the Sunday nudge is
device-local as well. **Today's logging rate may be measuring a device bug, not
creator apathy**, and every insight built on top of it inherits the defect.

**4. I under-ranked §7 by a wide margin.** Three of four seats moved the monthly
algorithm note into the top three: no data dependency, visible to a day-1 user,
small effort, and the only item that produces a *public* artifact. One
correction the council caught that I missed — **a creator-facing Algorithm Intel
tab already existed and was deliberately removed** ("strategic insights are
moving inline," `index.html:9650`, JSX preserved commented at `13813`). So the
right form is a public dated changelog plus an email, **not** a restored tab.

**5. `robots.txt` blocks the crawlers that gate every AI-citation benefit these
features could produce.** `OAI-SearchBot` and `ChatGPT-User` are both
`Disallow: /` (`robots.txt:22-26`). The file's own header already articulates the
correct principle for Perplexity — *"retrieval (search + answer), not model
training"* — and then fails to apply it to OpenAI's retrieval agents.
`Claude-SearchBot` / `Claude-User` aren't listed at all. This is a one-line
inconsistency with the file's own stated policy, and it costs nothing to fix.
(Confirmed for this repo's host; verify whatever serves the marketing pages.)

### Where the council split — and the resolution

§1 (business-outcome logging) drew the sharpest disagreement:

- **Demand-gen:** the strongest marketable claim on the list. "Tracks DMs and
  consults booked, not views" is a comparison row no competitor can check.
- **Head of content:** agents structurally cannot attribute a close to a post —
  that inability is *why* they can't justify content spend. The field will be
  blank for everyone except power users who were never going to churn.
- **Brand:** it is the first unfalsifiable number in the product. Every figure
  VIRL states today is either OCR'd from the creator's own analytics or blocked.
  This one is a human guess about causality, stored as an integer,
  indistinguishable from a measurement downstream. And since creators log wins
  and skip flops, the Scorecard would grade a biased sample and say "3 of 4 hit."

**Resolution:** ship the field, never launder it. Store it flagged as
creator-attested, quote it back in the creator's frame ("you logged 6
conversations that started from social — your call, not my math"), render it
visually distinct from OCR'd numbers, and never aggregate it into a VIRL causal
claim. **Lock this now, before anyone writes a landing page: VIRL never says it
caused a business outcome.** Not "VIRL got you 6 consults." Critically, do *not*
make this field the foundation of the insight layer — see the next item.

### Build the insight layer on behavior, not on typing

The best constructive idea to come out of the council, and it fixes §3's real
problem. VIRL already collects a complete per-card behavioral funnel, free, for
100% of users: `vault_saved`, `script_generated` (costs 2 credits — a costly
signal is an honest signal), `caption_generated`, `caption_remixed`,
`post_marked`, `plan_partial_regen` (rejected this card), `draft_edited`
(rewrote it), `plan_calendar_exported`.

Bucket **those** by pillar / funnel stage / hook archetype. The buckets fill an
order of magnitude faster than logged results, they work for every user
including non-loggers, and the drop-off between stages is itself the insight —
"you generate 14 cards a week and script 3" is more actionable than any view
count. Engagement data overlays on top when it exists, rather than being the
foundation.

This also defuses a statistical problem the brand seat caught: dimensioning
multiplies bucket count roughly tenfold against gates calibrated for two
dimensions (`MIN_PER_FORMAT = 2`, `performance-insights.js:26-29`). At n=2 per
bucket, §3 as written ships noise in a confident first-person voice. Raise the
per-dimension floor, require a minimum effect size, and **drop the example claim
at §3** — *"convert posts only land when a nurture post ran within 48 hours"* is
an interaction effect a creator posting 5×/week will not power in a year. Don't
spec a headline the data layer can never honestly earn.

### Other binding revisions

- **§2 must grade VIRL's bet, not the creator.** The `success_metric` thresholds
  are model-invented (`prompts.js:1501`). Telling a creator on Monday that she
  missed a target VIRL made up is both dishonest and off-persona. The Scorecard
  must be able to say *"I set that target too high."* That version is what
  actually sells the strategist positioning — the strategist owns the bet.
  Also: my sample verdict copy in §2 violates `RATIONALE_RULES` (it invents a
  causal mechanism and cites no input). The sample is the spec; fix it first.
- **§2 needs its failure case and its empty state written in the same PR.** A
  verdict never ships without the next move attached in the same sentence. A
  Monday surface whose default is "not enough data" is a weekly reminder that
  the product doesn't know.
- **§4 should offer a swap, not ask a question.** "Why didn't you post this?"
  gets a polite lie — *didn't fit my week* and *ran out of time* are the same
  socially-costless answer and will absorb most clicks. Instead: when a card
  goes unshipped, offer *"want me to swap this one?"* → b-roll version /
  carousel / different topic. The choice **is** the skip reason, cleanly
  labeled, with no admission required, and it produces a card they might
  actually ship. Better still, infer the pattern and confirm it in one tap.
- **§5: keep it, don't market it.** "Tightened 3 cards to your voice" advertises
  that the flagship surface's first pass was off-voice. Disclose per-card
  ("rewrote this one — it was drifting"), never as a plan-level banner, and keep
  it off every landing page. It's claim protection, not claim creation.
- **§6 cut to its defensible sliver.** Compounding-voice memory is contested
  table stakes — competitors already market "knowledge base that compounds" and
  "Content DNA" (council-sourced, not independently verified). The ownable part
  is that VIRL's is **visible and editable**, and that's ~20% of the work. Seed
  it on day 1 from `voiceSamples` + `handleResearch` rather than treating it as
  a month-6 accumulator. Fold it into Creator Profile as a "What I've learned"
  section — a second "Profile" the user didn't write is a support ticket.
- **Naming: "Scorecard" and "Voice Profile" both die.** "Scorecard" is dashboard
  vocabulary that grades the creator, on the axis this document argues is free
  inside Instagram. Use an eyebrow — **Last week** — and let VIRL talk. Existing
  convention: `VIRL <thing>` for surfaces where VIRL generates, plain nouns for
  the creator's things. Everything here is the creator's, so none of it takes
  the VIRL prefix.
- **Make the artifacts shareable.** Three seats flagged this independently: the
  weekly read is the most screenshot-worthy object this roadmap creates, and it
  is trapped in-app. A public permalink with a "planned with VIRL" footer turns
  a retention feature into a distribution one, and wires into the referral
  engine (`api/_lib/referrals.js`) that already exists.
- **Trial length is a config change against the actual bottleneck.** If value
  needs three Mondays, a 14-day trial cannot deliver it. 21 days, or
  calendar-align the trial.

### Correction: the two core guarantees got demoted, and one is leaking today

The council optimized for proving value and getting found. Both are right, and
in taking their advice the revised order pushed the voice gate to last and never
audited platform fidelity at all. Those two properties *are* the product. A
per-surface audit of both:

**Voice — near-universal.** `buildVoiceBlocks` reaches every generation surface;
`plan_partial` inherits it through `fullBuilt.systemPrompt`. (`plan_strategy`
sits outside `VOICE_LEARNING_TYPES`, which is correct — strategy prose is VIRL's
register, not the creator's.) The one hole is the known one: Plan measures drift
and never acts on it. It does not belong last.

**Platform best practice — plan-heavy, and it falls off a cliff elsewhere.**

| Mechanic | Plan | Partial regen | Strategy regen | Caption | Scan | Script / Long / Blog |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Peak times | yes | no | no | no | no | no |
| Hashtag count | yes | no | — | yes | no | — |
| Caption limit | yes | — | — | yes | no | — |
| Format priority | yes | no | no | no | yes | yes |
| Cadence | yes | — | — | — | — | — |
| Trends | yes | no | no | yes | yes | no |

Four defects behind that table:

1. **Partial regen obeys a playbook it cannot see.** `buildPlanPartial` writes
   its own userPrompt with neither `playbookCtx` nor `trendsCtx`, while
   instructing the model that *"Hashtag arrays still follow the platform's
   playbook hashtag_count"* (`prompts.js:1627`). The parent plan's
   `trendsSnapshot` is threaded into the builder, used to derive the cached
   system prompt via `buildPlan`, and then dropped with that call's userPrompt.
   Every regenerated card loses peak-time anchoring, format priority, hashtag
   count, and trends. This is a bug, not a gap, and regen is a 1-credit action
   creators use constantly.
2. **`peak_times` reaches exactly one surface in the product** — rendered only
   by `platformPlaybookBlock` (`prompts.js:204`) via `planPlaybookContext`. Scan
   delivers a verdict ("I'd post this one — my call: Instagram") and never says
   when, with the data sitting right there in the playbook.
3. **Scan invents its own hashtag and caption rules.** Its schema asks for
   hashtags *"count sized to post_type"* against a static in-prompt table
   (`prompts.js:2118`); `scanPlaybookContext` passes only `top_signals` and
   `format_priority`, so the live `hashtag_count` and `caption_limit` never
   arrive.
4. **`script`, `long_post`, and `blog_post` get no trend context** — and no
   explicit empty state either, so nothing holds the model back from reaching
   into stale training data. Plan's `TREND_SOURCE_RULE` pattern
   (`prompts.js:320`) exists precisely to prevent this and isn't applied here.

Also worth a decision rather than a default: the playbook's `cadence` is
advisory, while the actual card count comes from `profile.postFreq × platform
count` (`computeCardRange`). Respecting the creator's capacity over the
platform's ideal is probably right — but it should be a stated call, since it
means "cadence best practice" is the one mechanic the creator's own setting
overrides.

### Revised order

**Tier 0 — integrity fixes. Small, and they protect the promise on surfaces
creators touch daily. These run alongside Gate 0, not after the roadmap.**

- Thread `playbookCtx` + `trendsCtx` into `buildPlanPartial`'s userPrompt (the
  data is already passed to the function and discarded) and into
  `buildPlanStrategy`.
- Feed `hashtagSlots`, `caption_limit`, and `peak_times` into Scan; add
  `peak_times` to Caption.
- Give `script` / `long_post` / `blog_post` either the trend block or the
  explicit no-trends empty state.
- Move the Plan voice repair (§5) up from last. It stays unmarketed — the
  council is right that it's claim protection, not a feature — but claim
  protection on the flagship surface is not a deferrable item.

**Gate 0 — before any roadmap item. Days, not weeks.**

1. `robots.txt`: unblock `OAI-SearchBot`, `ChatGPT-User`, and add
   `Claude-SearchBot` / `Claude-User`. Keep the training crawlers blocked — that
   is the file's own stated policy, applied consistently.
2. Server-side posted state. The logging funnel may be broken rather than
   ignored, and nothing downstream is trustworthy until this is known.
3. Query `result_logged : plan_generated` by week-of-tenure. Half a day.

**Then re-decide.** If logging is healthy once posted state travels, items 1–4
proceed as revised. If it isn't, pivot the insight layer to behavioral signal
and drop most of §1/§3 as originally specced.

**Then, in this order:**

