## PM AUDIT — supersedes the fix ordering above

Run with `.claude/skills/virl-product-review`, one auditor per half of the
promise, each told to treat this document as unverified and hunt for what it
missed. Both found defects larger than anything above. **Answer to "will the
fixes above make VIRL a true strategist that writes in the user's voice": no.**
They are necessary, they are not sufficient, and they were not aimed at the
three biggest problems — none of which appear anywhere earlier in this document.

### The finding that changes the order: voice exemplars are in the wrong register

`saveToVault` stores both fields on every vault row: `description` — VIRL's
strategic one-liner — at `index.html:11500`, and `caption` — the creator's actual
publishable copy — at `index.html:11525`. `buildExemplarPool` then reads:

```js
const description = v.description || v.text || null;   // vault-exemplars.js:91
```

`v.caption` is never read. `description` is always populated (the plan schema
requires it, `prompts.js:1256`), so the fallback never fires. The renderer then
labels it for the model:

```js
if (e.description) body.push("Caption: " + …);          // vault-exemplars.js:189
```

`description` is explicitly VIRL's register — it sits in the VIRL column of
`PLAN_REGISTER_SPLIT` (`prompts.js:1090`) and is specified as *"ONE sentence
pitch, MAX 20 words — the angle / why this post exists"* (`prompts.js:1503`).

So the block introduced to the model as *"these tell you what 'sounds right' for
THIS creator better than any abstract voice description"* (`prompts.js:46`) is
feeding it **VIRL's own strategist prose, labelled as the creator's caption.**
The creator's real caption is in the row and never read. Reach: every surface
receiving `vaultPatterns` — plan, plan_partial, script, caption, caption_remix,
long_post, blog_post. The results path repeats it (`index.html:11607`) and ranks
those items *higher* (`vault-exemplars.js:135`), so the posted-and-logged
exemplars — the strongest by design — are the most contaminated.

It compounds with use. The more a creator saves, the more of VIRL's register is
fed back as their voice. This is a mechanism by which output drifts toward VIRL
and away from the creator, specifically for the most engaged users.

**And it corrupts the enforcement.** `exemplarsAsVoiceText`
(`vault-exemplars.js:200`) pulls the same `hook + description` pair into
`buildVoiceReference` (`chat.js:187`), which is the reference corpus for
`computeVoiceDrift` and therefore for the corrective-retry gate. The gate meant
to protect the creator's voice is calibrated partly on VIRL's register.

**Consequence for sequencing: do not ship the Tier 0 voice repair first.**
Enforcing harder against a contaminated reference pushes output further toward
VIRL. Fix `vault-exemplars.js:91` before any voice enforcement work. It is
roughly two lines and it repairs the exemplar block, the drift reference, and
the drift gate at once, across eight surfaces.

### The finding that guts the strategist half: trend enforcement is dead code

`[TREND-VERIFY]` (`index.html:11124-11157`) is the only place in the product
where a model claim is checked against real data instead of trusted. It builds
its allow-list by walking `Object.keys(freshSnapshot)` expecting the **legacy**
shape `{ [platform]: { items: [{ trend }] } }`. The shape actually sent is v2
(`trend-context.js:239`): `{ v, block, segment, refreshedAt, items }`. None of
those values carries an `.items` array — `freshSnapshot.items` *is* the array,
and arrays have no `.items`. So `knownTrends` is always empty, the
`knownTrends.length > 0` guard never passes, and the scrub never runs. The item
field was also renamed `trend` → `display_name`, so a shape fix alone wouldn't
restore it.

Nothing has stripped an invented trend from a plan since the v2 migration. The
design rationale at `prompts.js:317` — *"a creator can verify a cited trend
against the in-app trends list"* — is exactly what this code existed to
guarantee.

It fails twice over, because the list the creator would check is a **different
dataset**. `cron/trends-refresh` still runs weekly into the legacy `trends`
table; generation reads `trend_items` via `buildTrendContext`. The legacy
table's only surviving consumer is the client's Algo tab. Live weekly vendor
spend is buying a display-only artifact that disagrees with what grounded the
plan.

And the freshness date is fabricated: `last_seen` appears in the trend query's
**filter** but not its **select** (`trend-context.js:151`), so `t.last_seen` is
undefined on every row, `latest` reduces to `""`, and `refreshedAt` falls
through to today (`:212`). A six-day-old trend is stamped as today's signal in
both the prompt and the UI (`index.html:13135`).

### Scan is the weakest surface in the product on both halves

It emits publishable creator copy — `SCAN_REGISTERS` (`prompts.js:2138`) assigns
hook, caption, overlay, slides, and hashtags to the creator — and:

- Receives **two of five voice blocks**. Broken twice independently:
  `scan_image`/`scan_video_frame` are absent from `VOICE_GEN_TYPES`
  (`chat.js:1174`) so vault patterns are never fetched, *and* both builders pass
  literal `null` for `vaultPatterns` anyway (`prompts.js:2168`, `:2192`) while
  taking the real one as an unused `_vaultPatterns`.
- Renders a platform verdict (*"My call: Instagram"*) with the creator's own
  strongest-platform data structurally unreachable — the exact line
  `performance-insights.js:152` produces.
- Sizes hashtags and captions from a static in-prompt table
  (`prompts.js:2114-2121`) while asserting *"within the platform's
  caption_limit"* — a value never passed.
- Solicits a trending-audio claim (`:2152`) with no trend-grounding rule, and
  can be entirely trend-dark when `profile.myPlatforms` is unset
  (`chat.js:780`).

### Other verified leaks not in this document

- **`TREND_SOURCE_RULE` reaches one surface.** It lives in `buildPlan`'s *user*
  prompt (`:1528`), so nothing inherits it. `captionTrendsContext` (`:338`) and
  `scanTrendsContext` (`:582`) render a block with no grounding rule and return
  silence when empty — the failure mode the comment at `:324` says must never
  happen. Tier 0 fixes script/long/blog and leaves caption and scan open.
- **`plan_strategy` inverts a stated precedence rule.** It receives
  `vaultPatterns`, discards it (`:1655`, `:1682`), and instructs that
  success_metric values follow the tier baseline (`:1714`) — while `buildPlan`
  (`:1501`) and the niche playbook (`niche-playbook.js:242`) both say logged
  results always override baselines. Regenerating your strategy silently
  replaces real targets with generic ones.
- **`plan_partial` loses more than Tier 0 accounts for**: also the niche success
  model, the industry format mix, and this week's business/listing context.
  Regenerate the card built around the creator's open house and the replacement
  doesn't know the open house exists.
- **The niche/industry layer never leaves the plan.** `formatNichePlaybookForPrompt`
  has two call sites, `getFormatGuidance` one. Nine surfaces have no
  how-content-converts-in-this-vertical grounding beyond optional profile fields.
- **The CI voice gate is blind to both voice leaks by construction.**
  `check-voice.mjs` passes `null` for `vaultPatterns` on every assertion
  (`:80`, `:133`, `:199`, `:225`), so it cannot observe the exemplar or
  performance blocks on any surface.
- **`plan_partial` is a second unenforced streaming surface** —
  `chat.js:1637` routes both it and `plan` through `handleStreamingPlan`.
- **The drift corpus mixes registers**: `VOICE_BEARING_KEYS`
  (`voice-drift.js:27`) includes `body`, which matches VIRL-voice
  `restDayTips[].body`, and omits creator-voice `title` and the `frames[]`
  fields.
- **The register split is asserted, never checked.** No runtime equivalent of
  `scrubCompliance` exists for voice. A caption saying "my call is Instagram"
  ships.
- **Playbook staleness is unbounded and refresh failure is silent.** Nothing
  reads `updated_at`; the admin email fires only on `drafted > 0`
  (`cron/playbook-refresh.js:166`), so "no changes found" and "broken for four
  months" are indistinguishable.

