## OPEN — revisit 2026-08-21: drift enforcement on the streaming surfaces

`plan` and `plan_partial` both stream (`chat.js`), and the corrective voice
retry runs only on the non-streaming path. Drift is computed, written to
`usage_events.drift_score_after`, and logged — then ignored.

**Deliberately deferred, not forgotten.** On 2026-08-07 the exemplar register
inversion was fixed, which means every drift score recorded before that date
was measured against a reference corpus containing VIRL's own strategist prose.
`DRIFT_RETRY_THRESHOLD = 55` was tuned on those numbers. Building enforcement
on a threshold derived from a contaminated baseline is how you end up
confidently rewriting good copy.

From 2026-08-07 forward the scores are clean. Around **2026-08-21**:

1. Pull `drift_score_after` from `usage_events` since the fix, split by
   `generationType`. Compare the enforced surfaces (caption, script,
   long_post, blog_post, scan) against the unenforced ones (plan,
   plan_partial).
2. Re-tune the threshold from the real distribution rather than inheriting 55.
3. Then decide on post-stream repair. Budget client work as well as server:
   `index.html`'s SSE parser handles only `text`, `meta`, `error`, `reset`. The
   server already emits a `compliance` event that nothing listens to, so
   accepting a post-stream correction needs a client handler, not just a
   server change. The three options are a full re-stream (doubles latency and
   cost on the most expensive surface), targeted per-card repair (cheapest,
   needs the client work), or hold-and-check before streaming (kills the
   perceived-latency win streaming exists for).

Also still open and unstarted: `TREND_SOURCE_RULE` reaches only the plan
surface; the niche/industry playbook never leaves the plan; static hashtag
tables on long_post and blog_post; unbounded playbook staleness with silent
refresh failure.

## The through-line

Every gap above is the same gap in a different costume:

**VIRL is excellent at deciding what to do and has almost no mechanism for
finding out whether it was right.**

The strategy is unscored. The success metrics are unloggable. The results are
one-dimensional. The skips are unexplained. The voice drift on the flagship
surface is measured and ignored. The learning window slides instead of
accumulating. The research is invisible.

Fix the loop and the existing prompt quality — which is genuinely strong —
starts compounding into something a creator can point at and justify. Leave it
open and VIRL stays a very good generator, competing on output quality in a
category where output quality is converging toward free.
